          <div class="archive-entry-title">The Words and the Work — Why Perceptual Jiu-Jitsu Exists</div>
          <div class="archive-entry-body">

          <p style="color:var(--gray-600);font-style:italic;font-size:14px;margin-bottom:2rem;">A Note Before We Begin</p>
          <p>This isn't the manual. The manual is long, and it's for people who want to teach this. This is the story of why it needed to exist in the first place.</p>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:28px;color:var(--white);letter-spacing:0.05em;margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">THE ITCH</h2>
          <p>When I started training, BJJ was a collection of techniques — moves, counters to moves, counters to counters. And it was fun. I was hooked on accumulating them, the way most people are early on.</p>
          <p>But somewhere in there, something started bothering me. Underneath all of it — every position, every escape, every sweep — there seemed to be something common. Something that was always there, doing the actual work, regardless of which technique I was looking at. I couldn't point to it. I didn't have a word for it. But I could feel that it was there, and that nobody was talking about it directly.</p>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:28px;color:var(--white);letter-spacing:0.05em;margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">BORROWED WORDS</h2>
          <p>Eventually I came across Rob Biernacki's work, and it gave me language for what I'd been sensing — alignment, frames, levers, wedges, base, posture, structure. Suddenly there were words for the thing underneath the thing.</p>
          <p>And here's the part that matters: those words sat with me for roughly a decade, and for most of that time, they were just words. They made sense. I could nod along, recognize what they were pointing at when I saw it. But I couldn't <em>do</em> anything with them. They weren't a curriculum. They weren't a way of teaching. They were a vocabulary for something I still couldn't operationalize.</p>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:28px;color:var(--white);letter-spacing:0.05em;margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">THE DOJO THAT NEEDED CONCEPTS</h2>
          <p>When I opened Third Place Dojo, I didn't want to teach the way I'd been taught — a long list of techniques, position by position. I wanted a curriculum built on concepts. Which sounded simple until I actually tried to do it, because it meant I couldn't just <em>use</em> the words anymore. I had to figure out what they actually meant, underneath themselves.</p>
          <p>What is base, really — not as a word, but as something a body does? What is a frame actually doing, mechanically, in the moment it's working? That's when things started connecting. Not because I learned new words, but because I finally had to find out what the old ones were standing on top of.</p>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:28px;color:var(--white);letter-spacing:0.05em;margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">PAST THE MAP</h2>
          <p>Once that started happening, it didn't stop at the original vocabulary. I started noticing connections between the concepts themselves — how they compound, how they depend on each other, why some things had to be felt rather than explained, why drilling some things too early produced nothing. That's the territory none of my sources had mapped. That's where Perceptual Jiu-Jitsu and the rest of what's documented here actually came from.</p>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:28px;color:var(--white);letter-spacing:0.05em;margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">WHAT THIS WAS ALWAYS ABOUT</h2>
          <p>At the bottom of all of it is one claim: BJJ is a contest of alignment under force. Both people are always doing two things — holding their own structure together while trying to take apart their partner's. The gap between those two is where everything else lives.</p>
          <p>Most training doesn't address that directly. It teaches the shapes — the techniques — and hopes that enough exposure eventually produces the underlying perception on its own. For some people it does. For a lot of people, it doesn't, and they spend years getting good at recognizing situations rather than feeling what's actually happening in them. The first kind of practitioner can walk into an unfamiliar gym against an unfamiliar body type and figure it out. The second kind gets lost, and usually doesn't know why.</p>
          <p>That difference isn't talent. It's what kind of thing got developed. And almost nothing in standard BJJ teaching — including the more modern, constraint-based approaches — addresses it on purpose.</p>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:28px;color:var(--white);letter-spacing:0.05em;margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">WHERE THIS STANDS</h2>
          <p>This is still a living framework. Parts of it are well-tested. Parts of it are honest hypotheses I'm still pressure-testing, including against people whose job is to find the holes in it. I'd rather say that plainly than pretend otherwise.</p>
          <p>But the question underneath all of it hasn't changed since it first started forming, before I had any of these words:</p>
          <blockquote>Did that move the gap?</blockquote>

